\f2\fs22 \cf3 Each FRQ has a defined focus the College Board publishes. Cramapple's archetype-by-archetype adaptation uses these names and definitions directly.
\f1\fs24 \cf0 \
\

\f2\fs22 \cf3 The College Board also operates paywalled, audit-gated resources: AP Classroom (unit guides, progress checks, question bank), the Teaching and Assessing video modules where master AP educators model instructional strategies, AP Summer Institutes (paid teacher training), and the AP teacher community. Orly should obtain audit-approved access where possible, as these resources inform expert validation and content authoring workflows that Cramapple cannot derive from public sources alone.
\f1\fs24 \cf0 \

\f2\fs22 \cf3 The published released scoring guidelines plus annotated sample student responses are the most operationally valuable public artifact. Each released FRQ comes with point-by-point commentary explaining why a response earned or missed each criterion. That is the closest the College Board comes to publishing a teaching standard for grading itself.
\f1\fs24 \cf0 \
\pard\pardeftab720\pardirnatural\partightenfactor0
\cf0 2.2 Academic Research on Test Preparation\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa120\pardirnatural\partightenfactor0

\f2\fs22 \cf3 The cognitive science of test preparation, instructional scaffolding, and short-window study has a substantial peer-reviewed literature. Cramapple draws on four streams.
\f1\fs24 \cf0 \

\f2\fs22 \cf3 The 2025 Hao et al. meta-analysis in Review of Educational Research is the most current synthesis of experimental studies on test preparation for large-scale educational tests. It finds that test prep does improve performance, but that effects vary with design. The single largest moderator is alignment with the assessed skills and constructs. This is the academic finding most directly supportive of Cramapple's canonical-content commitment.
\f1\fs24 \cf0 \

\f2\fs22 \cf3 Older meta-analyses on coaching for cognitive ability tests find consistent but modest effects, with overall effect sizes around d = 0.27. Coaching combined with practice produces larger effects than practice alone; prior test-taking experience amplifies coaching effects.
\f1\fs24 \cf0 \

\f2\fs22 \cf3 The worked-example and faded-scaffolding literature (Sweller, Renkl, Atkinson, and others) finds medium-to-large effect sizes for worked examples followed by progressively faded versions, particularly for novices. Effect sizes for procedural and conceptual knowledge in physics are around d = 0.7 and d = 0.58 respectively. The literature also flags the expertise reversal effect: worked examples that help novices can slow more advanced learners.
\f1\fs24 \cf0 \

\f2\fs22 \cf3 The retrieval-practice and spaced-repetition literature (Karpicke, Roediger) establishes that testing oneself on material produces substantially stronger retention than re-reading, particularly when retrieval is distributed over time. PEDAGOGY.docx contains the deeper treatment.
\f1\fs24 \cf0 \
\pard\pardeftab720\pardirnatural\partightenfactor0
\cf0 2.3 Academic Research on AI Essay Grading\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa120\pardirnatural\partightenfactor0

\f2\fs22 \cf3 A second academic stream is more recent and directly relevant: empirical studies of large language model grading of student writing. This literature has matured rapidly in the last two years and now informs specific design decisions about how Cramapple grades FRQs.
\f1\fs24 \cf0 \

\f2\fs22 \cf3 Tate et al. (2024) compared GPT-3.5 and GPT-4 holistic scoring of approximately 1,800 secondary student essays against human raters. Findings: weighted Cohen's Kappa with humans was around .52 for GPT-3.5 and .58 for GPT-4, while human-human agreement was around .79\'96.82. AI was more internally consistent than humans (AI-AI exact agreement above 80% vs human-human at 43%). AI was within one point of human scores 89% of the time vs humans at 74%. AI compressed the range (fewer extreme scores) and was less consistent with humans on English Learner writing. Conclusion: appropriate for low-stakes formative use, not summative.
\f1\fs24 \cf0 \

\f2\fs22 \cf3 Se\'dfler et al. (2025) tested LLMs on multidimensional scoring of German essays evaluated by 37 teachers across 10 criteria. LLMs gave systematically higher scores than teachers, particularly on content quality. A companion paper found that simplified rubrics performed as well as detailed rubrics for three of four LLMs \'97 a cost-control finding.
\f1\fs24 \cf0 \

\f2\fs22 \cf3 Zacharis & Papadakis (2025) compared ChatGPT-4o against two faculty raters on 91 Greek essays using a 9-criterion rubric. Human-human ICC was .884; AI-human ICCs were .279\'96.406. AI inflated scores by 2.7\'963.3 points on a 90-point scale, compressed variance (SD 8.4 vs human 10.7\'9611.5), and exhibited proportional bias (over-scored weak essays, under-scored strong ones). Grade-band agreement was 40\'9651%. Principal component analysis suggested AI captured a narrower construct of writing quality than humans.
\f1\fs24 \cf0 \

\f2\fs22 \cf3 The consensus across this literature: AI grading is appropriate for formative, low-stakes use under human oversight. Cramapple's use case is squarely within the formative-prep window the literature endorses. The literature also identifies specific failure modes that any AI grading system inherits and must design against. These are catalogued in Section 5.5.
\f1\fs24 \cf0 \
\pard\pardeftab720\pardirnatural\partightenfactor0
\cf0 2.4 AP Teacher Practice\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa120\pardirnatural\partightenfactor0

\f2\fs22 \cf3 A fourth source of teaching authority is the body of practice developed by experienced AP teachers, articulated in public-facing materials by AP teacher trainers, tutoring practices, and AP teacher-bloggers. While less formally peer-reviewed than the academic literature, it has high operational value because it has been developed and refined against the actual exam by people who score AP responses.
\f1\fs24 \cf0 \

\f2\fs22 \cf3 Four practices recur across credible AP teacher sources:
\f1\fs24 \cf0 \
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\sa60\pardirnatural\partightenfactor0
\ls2\ilvl0
\f4\i\b\fs22 \cf3 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\ul \strike \strikec3 Command-verb decoding. Students underline the command verbs (explain, calculate, identify, justify, predict) before writing. The verb determines what kind of evidence the rubric awards points for.
\f1\i0\b0\fs24 \cf0 \ulnone \strike0\striked0 \
\ls2\ilvl0
\f4\i\b\fs22 \cf3 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\ul \strike \strikec3 Rubric-criterion internalization. Students self-grade and peer-grade against the actual rubric. The repetition builds an internal model of what does and does not earn a point.
\f1\i0\b0\fs24 \cf0 \ulnone \strike0\striked0 \
\ls2\ilvl0
\f4\i\b\fs22 \cf3 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\ul \strike \strikec3 Scoring precision. Concise responses that hit each rubric criterion directly outscore long, discursive responses that bury the criterion. Train students to write to the rubric, not around it.
\f1\i0\b0\fs24 \cf0 \ulnone \strike0\striked0 \
\ls2\ilvl0
\f4\i\b\fs22 \cf3 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\ul \strike \strikec3 Pattern recognition. FRQ types repeat across exam years. Master teachers train students to recognize the archetype within seconds and to deploy the corresponding response structure.
\f1\i0\b0\fs24 \cf0 \ulnone \strike0\striked0 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa120\pardirnatural\partightenfactor0
\cf0 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sb80\sa200\pardirnatural\partightenfactor0
\cf0 \
\pard\pardeftab720\pardirnatural\partightenfactor0
\cf0 3. The Score Loop\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa120\pardirnatural\partightenfactor0

\f2\fs22 \cf3 Every Cramapple teaching interaction follows the same four-beat pattern. The shape stays constant. The content within each beat adapts to the question archetype and the student's diagnosed gap.
\f1\fs24 \cf0 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa120\pardirnatural\partightenfactor0

\f5\fs22 \cf2 Frame \uc0\u8594  Attempt \u8594  Score + Diagnose \u8594  Repair
\f1\fs24 \cf0 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa120\pardirnatural\partightenfactor0

\f2\fs22 \cf3 Followed by a Lock step that connects the interaction to the spaced-retrieval engine.
\f1\fs24 \cf0 \
\pard\pardeftab720\pardirnatural\partightenfactor0
\cf0 3.1 Frame\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa120\pardirnatural\partightenfactor0

\f2\fs22 \cf3 Before the student attempts anything, Cramapple names what is being tested and what earns points. The Frame step is short \'97 typically 1\'962 sentences \'97 and renders at the top of the question screen. It sets the rubric expectation explicitly.
\f1\fs24 \cf0 \

\f2\fs22 \cf3 Frame content by question type:
\f1\fs24 \cf0 \
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\sa60\pardirnatural\partightenfactor0
\ls3\ilvl0
\f4\i\b\fs22 \cf3 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\ul \strike \strikec3 FRQ: "This is a 4-point question. Command verb: explain. The rubric awards points for naming the mechanism, identifying the affected structure, and predicting the outcome."
\f1\i0\b0\fs24 \cf0 \ulnone \strike0\striked0 \
\ls3\ilvl0
\f4\i\b\fs22 \cf3 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\ul \strike \strikec3 MCQ: "This question tests gene regulation. The common trap is confusing induction with repression."
\f1\i0\b0\fs24 \cf0 \ulnone \strike0\striked0 \
\ls3\ilvl0
\f4\i\b\fs22 \cf3 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\ul \strike \strikec3 Quantitative: "This calculation uses the chi-square formula. Points are awarded for setup, computation, and stating the conclusion with respect to the null hypothesis."
\f1\i0\b0\fs24 \cf0 \ulnone \strike0\striked0 \
\ls3\ilvl0
\f4\i\b\fs22 \cf3 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\ul \strike \strikec3 Data interpretation: "The prompt asks you to identify a trend in the data and predict what happens at an unstated value. Points are awarded for the trend description and the predicted value with units."
\f1\i0\b0\fs24 \cf0 \ulnone \strike0\striked0 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa120\pardirnatural\partightenfactor0

\f2\fs22 \cf3 Frame draws directly on AP teacher practice (command-verb decoding, rubric-criterion internalization) and on the rule-based coaching finding in the academic literature.
\f1\fs24 \cf0 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa80\pardirnatural\partightenfactor0

\f3\i\fs18 \cf4 Decision: Frame is always present by default. A future Hypothesis is that students approaching mastery may find it noisy and benefit from a collapsed-by-default Frame.
\f1\i0\fs24 \cf0 \
\pard\pardeftab720\pardirnatural\partightenfactor0
\cf0 3.2 Attempt\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa120\pardirnatural\partightenfactor0

\f2\fs22 \cf3 The student does the work. For FRQ, the system captures the full reasoning the student writes, not just the final answer. For MCQ, it captures the choice plus an optional one-line "why" \'97 that one line is what enables a precise misconception diagnosis at the Score step. For quantitative work, the system captures intermediate steps.
\f1\fs24 \cf0 \

\f2\fs22 \cf3 The Attempt step is deliberately minimal in scaffolding. The product's commitment is that the student is doing the cognitive work; Cramapple is not generating answers on the student's behalf. This is consistent with the vision's Section 4.2 commitment that Cramapple is not a homework answer generator.
\f1\fs24 \cf0 \

\f2\fs22 \cf3 The Attempt step is also where Cramapple captures the metadata used by the integrity signals described in Section 9.5 (keystroke timing patterns, paste events).
\f1\fs24 \cf0 \
\pard\pardeftab720\pardirnatural\partightenfactor0
\cf0 3.3 Score + Diagnose\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa120\pardirnatural\partightenfactor0

\f2\fs22 \cf3 Cramapple evaluates the response criterion-by-criterion, not holistically. For FRQ, the system returns the 9-element feedback structure committed to in the vision (Section 6.2): points earned out of available, criteria satisfied, criteria not satisfied, evidence from the response, smallest improvement likely to earn the next point, an improved answer, a complete top-level answer, an error type, and a recommended next action.
\f1\fs24 \cf0 \

\f2\fs22 \cf3 The grading itself uses a multi-step rubric-aligned chain-of-thought workflow rather than a single prompt. The decomposition is: identify the question archetype and rubric criteria; for each criterion, extract evidence from the student's response (or note its absence); for each criterion, score with brief rationale; generate the minimum-fix content; aggregate. This pipeline is the academic consensus architecture and is what competitive grading products like Fiveable have publicly committed to as well.
\f1\fs24 \cf0 \

\f2\fs22 \cf3 The diagnostic move selects the Repair mode. Errors are classified into types \'97 missing knowledge, incorrect mechanism, insufficient specificity, unsupported claim, misread question, data interpretation error, formula setup error, arithmetic error, contradiction. Each error type maps to a default Repair mode (Section 4.4).
\f1\fs24 \cf0 \
\pard\pardeftab720\pardirnatural\partightenfactor0
\cf0 3.4 Repair\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa120\pardirnatural\partightenfactor0

\f2\fs22 \cf3 Repair is where the specific gap that lost the point gets closed. Three sub-modes are available; the diagnosis selects which one. Each mode is grounded in a distinct research base and is appropriate for a distinct kind of gap. Treated in full in Section 4.
\f1\fs24 \cf0 \
\pard\pardeftab720\pardirnatural\partightenfactor0
\cf0 3.5 The Lock\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa120\pardirnatural\partightenfactor0

\f2\fs22 \cf3 After Repair, the missed point enters a spaced-retrieval queue. The student encounters the same skill in a slightly different surface form 24\'9672 hours later. Treated in Section 5.
\f1\fs24 \cf0 \
\
\pard\pardeftab720\sb80\sa200\pardirnatural\partightenfactor0
\cf0 \
\pard\pardeftab720\pardirnatural\partightenfactor0
\cf0 4. The Three Repair Modes\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa120\pardirnatural\partightenfactor0

\f2\fs22 \cf3 Repair is the most expensive step in the loop in terms of student time and inference cost, and the most pedagogically consequential. Selecting the right Repair mode for the diagnosed gap is what differentiates effective tutoring from generic feedback.
\f1\fs24 \cf0 \
\pard\pardeftab720\pardirnatural\partightenfactor0
\cf0 4.1 Tighten\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa120\pardirnatural\partightenfactor0

\f2\fs22 \cf3 Used when the student has the underlying concept but missed the rubric language, skipped a causal step, or wrote imprecisely. The student needs a small, targeted correction, not a re-teach.
\f1\fs24 \cf0 \

\f2\fs22 \cf3 In practice: Cramapple identifies the specific gap in the response and prompts the student to attempt the missing piece. For a causal-explanation FRQ where the student's chain skipped a mechanism step, Cramapple may render an inline bracket marker (a "[...]" placeholder) at the exact location of the gap and pose a Socratic prompt that points at the missing token.
\f1\fs24 \cf0 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa120\pardirnatural\partightenfactor0

\f0\b\fs22 \cf3 Example. 
\f2\b0 Student writes: "High heat causes the cell to lose its function." Cramapple renders: "High heat [...] causes the cell to lose its function." Prompt: "You named the stressor and the outcome. The rubric wants the mechanism. What happens to the tertiary protein bonds when kinetic energy exceeds baseline limits?"
\f1\fs24 \cf0 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa80\pardirnatural\partightenfactor0

\f3\i\fs18 \cf4 Source: Blueprint_Teaching draft (Logic-Gap Protocol and Bracket Marker UI). One specific UI expression of the vision's commitment to "smallest improvement likely to earn the next point." The sentence-level visual highlighting approach is also the convergent UX across competitive AI feedback tools (GPTZero, EssayGrader), validating the direction.
\f1\i0\fs24 \cf0 \
\pard\pardeftab720\pardirnatural\partightenfactor0
\cf0 4.2 Show\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa120\pardirnatural\partightenfactor0

\f2\fs22 \cf3 Used when the student is missing knowledge, missing a method, or has a misconception. The student needs to see how a high-scoring response is constructed, then practice it.
\f1\fs24 \cf0 \

\f2\fs22 \cf3 Show is a faded worked-example sequence. The student first sees a complete worked example with all steps shown and annotated against the rubric criteria. Then a faded version of the same problem with the last step removed. Then a parallel problem on the same skill with different surface content, which the student solves independently.
\f1\fs24 \cf0 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa120\pardirnatural\partightenfactor0

\f0\b\fs22 \cf3 Example. 
\f5\b0 For a chi-square calculation the student missed: Cramapple shows a complete worked example with each step labeled (state hypothesis \uc0\u8594  compute expected values \u8594  compute chi-square statistic \u8594  compare to critical value \u8594  state conclusion). Then a problem with the conclusion step removed. Then a parallel problem on different content.
\f1\fs24 \cf0 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa80\pardirnatural\partightenfactor0

\f3\i\fs18 \cf4 Source: Sweller, Renkl, Atkinson, and the cognitive load / worked-example literature. Meta-analytic effect sizes around d = 0.6\'960.7 for procedural and conceptual knowledge. Fading the last step first ("backward fading") is the empirically preferred pattern.
\f1\i0\fs24 \cf0 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa80\pardirnatural\partightenfactor0

\f5\fs18 \cf4 Decision: Show defaults to a three-step sequence (full \uc0\u8594  faded \u8594  parallel). The number of faded intermediate steps is a Hypothesis to be tested with usage data.
\f1\fs24 \cf0 \
\pard\pardeftab720\pardirnatural\partightenfactor0
\cf0 4.3 Stretch\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa120\pardirnatural\partightenfactor0

\f2\fs22 \cf3 Used when the student earned the point cleanly. Same skill, slightly harder context, different unit content. The goal is to confirm that the win was a real skill acquisition and not pattern-matching to a familiar surface, and to build transfer.
\f1\fs24 \cf0 \

\f2\fs22 \cf3 Stretch is the only Repair mode that exists without a "gap." It is a deliberate post-success move that the vision commits to in Section 6.3.
\f1\fs24 \cf0 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa80\pardirnatural\partightenfactor0

\f3\i\fs18 \cf4 Source: Worked-example literature on near and far transfer (Rayner et al., Renkl); the College Board's explicit design intent in the CED that science practices "spiral throughout the course."
\f1\i0\fs24 \cf0 \
\pard\pardeftab720\pardirnatural\partightenfactor0
