\cf0 5. The Lock and Calibration\
5.1 The Lock \'97 Spaced Retrieval\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa120\pardirnatural\partightenfactor0

\f2\fs22 \cf3 Repair closes the gap in the moment. The Lock is what makes the closure durable.
\f1\fs24 \cf0 \

\f2\fs22 \cf3 After a Repair completes, the underlying skill (defined as the Science Practice + Unit + sub-skill combination) is registered in the student's retrieval queue with a return interval of 24\'9672 hours. The student will encounter the same skill again \'97 in a different question with different surface content \'97 at the next session that lands within the return window.
\f1\fs24 \cf0 \

\f2\fs22 \cf3 The Lock is the mechanism that translates a single repaired point into durable performance. The retrieval-practice literature is unusually consistent on this: distributed retrieval produces substantially stronger retention than re-reading or massed practice. The 10-day cram window is short enough that we cannot space retrieval across weeks, but it is more than long enough to support 24\'9672 hour intervals.
\f1\fs24 \cf0 \

\f2\fs22 \cf3 Lock is also what enables the next-best-action engine's recommendations. When Cramapple recommends a session focused on a specific weakness, it is selecting from the Lock queue rather than from generic curriculum coverage.
\f1\fs24 \cf0 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa80\pardirnatural\partightenfactor0

\f3\i\fs18 \cf4 Open: The exact return-interval algorithm. The working default is 24 hours for a missed point and 48\'9672 hours for a Stretch-confirmed point.
\f1\i0\fs24 \cf0 \
\pard\pardeftab720\pardirnatural\partightenfactor0
\cf0 5.2 Inter-Rater Agreement Target\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa120\pardirnatural\partightenfactor0

\f2\fs22 \cf3 The literature on AI essay grading converges on weighted Cohen's Kappa as the appropriate metric for inter-rater agreement. It accounts for both chance agreement and the ordinal nature of scoring, and it is what every empirical study in this space uses.
\f1\fs24 \cf0 \

\f2\fs22 \cf3 Reference points from the published literature: human-human agreement on rubric-based scoring is typically .79\'96.82 weighted Kappa; the best published AI-human results (Tate 2024 with GPT-4 on holistic scoring) reached .58. Cramapple's case is structurally easier than these benchmarks for three reasons: criterion-level (analytic) scoring is more tractable than holistic; AP Biology rubrics are more constrained than open essay prompts; and Cramapple's grading is wrapped in a teaching system that does not depend on grading accuracy alone for value.
\f1\fs24 \cf0 \

\f2\fs22 \cf3 Decision: launch gate is weighted Cohen's Kappa of at least .60 against expert AP-reader grading on a held-out validation set of released FRQs. Aspirational target is .70. This puts Cramapple at parity-to-better than Fiveable's published "within half a point of readers" claim (roughly equivalent to .55\'96.60 weighted Kappa depending on the rubric structure) and above the best published academic results on harder grading tasks.
\f1\fs24 \cf0 \
\pard\pardeftab720\pardirnatural\partightenfactor0
\cf0 5.3 Known Failure Modes and Calibration Moves\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa120\pardirnatural\partightenfactor0

\f2\fs22 \cf3 Three peer-reviewed studies of AI essay grading (Tate 2024, Se\'dfler 2025, Zacharis & Papadakis 2025) document a consistent set of failure modes that any LLM-based grader inherits. Cramapple's grader will exhibit these by default. The calibration architecture must address them explicitly.
\f1\fs24 \cf0 \
\pard\pardeftab720\pardirnatural\partightenfactor0
\cf0 Range compression\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa120\pardirnatural\partightenfactor0

\f2\fs22 \cf3 Universal finding across all three empirical studies. AI grades cluster toward the middle of the scale, with fewer extreme scores than humans assign. In Zacharis 2025, AI scores had a standard deviation of 8.4 vs humans at 10.7\'9611.5 on the same 90-point rubric. In Tate 2024, AI gave fewer 1s and 6s than humans on a 1\'966 scale. This compresses the discriminative power of the score.
\f1\fs24 \cf0 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa120\pardirnatural\partightenfactor0

\f0\b\fs22 \cf3 Design move: 
\f2\b0 Anchor examples included in the rubric package. For each FRQ archetype, the grader is given two to three worked examples spanning the score range \'97 a full-credit response, a middle-credit response, and a low-credit response \'97 annotated against the rubric criteria. This widens the implicit reference range the model considers when scoring.
\f1\fs24 \cf0 \
\pard\pardeftab720\pardirnatural\partightenfactor0
\cf0 Score inflation\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa120\pardirnatural\partightenfactor0

\f2\fs22 \cf3 Documented in all three empirical studies. AI assigns systematically higher scores than human raters. Zacharis 2025 measured 2.7\'963.3 points of inflation on a 90-point scale. Se\'dfler 2025 found LLMs gave higher scores than 37 German teachers across 10 criteria, particularly on content quality. Tate 2024 found GPT-4 means above human means.
\f1\fs24 \cf0 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa120\pardirnatural\partightenfactor0

\f0\b\fs22 \cf3 Design move: 
\f2\b0 Test for inflation explicitly in the launch validation. Compare the distribution of Cramapple grader scores to known distributions of human scores on the same released FRQs. If a consistent positive bias is detected, calibrate it down via prompt adjustment or post-hoc correction before launch.
\f1\fs24 \cf0 \
\pard\pardeftab720\pardirnatural\partightenfactor0
\cf0 Proportional bias (regression to the mean)\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa120\pardirnatural\partightenfactor0

\f2\fs22 \cf3 Zacharis 2025 demonstrated via Bland-Altman analysis that AI over-scored weak essays and under-scored strong essays, with a statistically significant negative regression slope. The AI effectively regresses all performances toward the middle. This is a fairness issue: weaker students are rewarded beyond merit, stronger students are not recognized for their excellence.
\f1\fs24 \cf0 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa120\pardirnatural\partightenfactor0

\f0\b\fs22 \cf3 Design move: 
\f2\b0 Detect this pattern in validation via Bland-Altman regression slope on the held-out set. If significant, the calibration response is the same anchor-example approach as for range compression \'97 giving the model explicit examples at the extremes reduces its tendency to pull toward the center.
\f1\fs24 \cf0 \
\pard\pardeftab720\pardirnatural\partightenfactor0
\cf0 Generic, rubric-recycled feedback\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa120\pardirnatural\partightenfactor0

\f2\fs22 \cf3 The dominant qualitative failure mode in the literature. Multiple studies note that AI feedback is "valued for coverage" but undermined by being "largely generic" \'97 often recycling phrases from rubric descriptors without specific essay-grounded evidence. Students perceive this as low quality even when scores are accurate.
\f1\fs24 \cf0 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa120\pardirnatural\partightenfactor0

\f0\b\fs22 \cf3 Design move: 
\f2\b0 The Repair-Tighten bracket-marker pattern and the criterion-specific minimum-fix language are explicit defenses against this. Every piece of feedback must quote or reference evidence from the student's actual response, not just paraphrase the rubric. Generic feedback is a bug to catch in evaluation, not a stylistic preference.
\f1\fs24 \cf0 \
\pard\pardeftab720\pardirnatural\partightenfactor0
\cf0 English Learner / equity bias\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa120\pardirnatural\partightenfactor0

\f2\fs22 \cf3 Tate 2024 documented worse AI-human agreement for English Learner writing (.40\'96.43 weighted Kappa) than non-EL writing (.51\'96.52). AP Biology FRQs are less language-dependent than English essays, so the effect is likely smaller in our domain, but it is not zero.
\f1\fs24 \cf0 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa120\pardirnatural\partightenfactor0

\f0\b\fs22 \cf3 Design move: 
\f2\b0 Validation must include responses from English Learners, and inter-rater agreement should be reported separately for EL and non-EL populations. If a meaningful gap exists, this is a launch blocker, not a release note.
\f1\fs24 \cf0 \
\pard\pardeftab720\pardirnatural\partightenfactor0
\cf0 5.4 Dual-Pass Grading for High-Value FRQs\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa120\pardirnatural\partightenfactor0

\f2\fs22 \cf3 The academic literature suggests that two independent grading passes followed by adjudication of disagreements improves accuracy. For 8\'9610 point long-form FRQs (AP Biology FRQ 1 and FRQ 2), the inference cost is likely worth it. For 4-point short FRQs (FRQ 3\'966), single-pass grading is probably sufficient.
\f1\fs24 \cf0 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa80\pardirnatural\partightenfactor0

\f3\i\fs18 \cf4 Hypothesis: dual-pass for FRQ 1 and FRQ 2, single-pass for FRQ 3\'966. To be validated against the launch-gate weighted Kappa target.
\f1\i0\fs24 \cf0 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa120\pardirnatural\partightenfactor0
\cf0 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sb80\sa200\pardirnatural\partightenfactor0
\cf0 \
\pard\pardeftab720\pardirnatural\partightenfactor0
\cf0 6. Adaptation by FRQ Archetype\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa120\pardirnatural\partightenfactor0

\f2\fs22 \cf3 The four-beat shape of the Score Loop does not change across question archetypes. What changes is the content of Frame and the default Repair menu.
\f1\fs24 \cf0 \
\pard\pardeftab720\pardirnatural\partightenfactor0
\cf0 6.1 FRQ 1 \'97 Experimental Analysis and Argumentation (long, 8\'9610 pts)\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa120\pardirnatural\partightenfactor0

\f2\fs22 \cf3 The longest and highest-value FRQ. Tests Science Practice 6 (Argumentation) heavily. Dual-pass grading default.
\f1\fs24 \cf0 \
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\sa60\pardirnatural\partightenfactor0
\ls4\ilvl0
\f4\i\b\fs22 \cf3 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\ul \strike \strikec3 Frame: Surface the experimental design (independent variable, dependent variable, controls), the rubric criteria for each sub-part, and the command verbs across sub-parts. Multi-part FRQs require multi-part Frames.
\f1\i0\b0\fs24 \cf0 \ulnone \strike0\striked0 \
\ls4\ilvl0
\f4\i\b\fs22 \cf3 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\ul \strike \strikec3 Repair: For an argumentation gap, default to Tighten with a focus on the claim\'96evidence\'96reasoning structure. For an experimental-design misreading, default to Show with a worked example of the design analysis.
\f1\i0\b0\fs24 \cf0 \ulnone \strike0\striked0 \
\pard\pardeftab720\pardirnatural\partightenfactor0
\cf0 6.2 FRQ 2 \'97 Graphing and Data Representation (long, 8\'9610 pts)\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa120\pardirnatural\partightenfactor0

\f2\fs22 \cf3 Tests Science Practices 4 and 5. Requires the student to construct a graph with proper axes, units, and scaling. Dual-pass grading default.
\f1\fs24 \cf0 \
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\sa60\pardirnatural\partightenfactor0
\ls5\ilvl0
\f4\i\b\fs22 \cf3 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\ul \strike \strikec3 Frame: Specify graph requirements (axes labeled, units, scale, data points plotted accurately, best-fit line where appropriate) as discrete criteria.
\f1\i0\b0\fs24 \cf0 \ulnone \strike0\striked0 \
\ls5\ilvl0
\f4\i\b\fs22 \cf3 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\ul \strike \strikec3 Repair: For a mechanical error, Tighten with a direct correction. For a misinterpretation, Show with a parallel data set worked out.
\f1\i0\b0\fs24 \cf0 \ulnone \strike0\striked0 \
\pard\pardeftab720\pardirnatural\partightenfactor0
\cf0 6.3 FRQ 3 \'97 Scientific Investigation (short, 4 pts)\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\sa60\pardirnatural\partightenfactor0
\ls6\ilvl0
\f4\i\b\fs22 \cf3 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\ul \strike \strikec3 Frame: Surface what constitutes a valid hypothesis, a controlled variable, and an appropriate measurement method for the system in question.
\f1\i0\b0\fs24 \cf0 \ulnone \strike0\striked0 \
\ls6\ilvl0
\f4\i\b\fs22 \cf3 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\ul \strike \strikec3 Repair: Show is the default \'97 most errors here are missing method knowledge rather than missing language.
\f1\i0\b0\fs24 \cf0 \ulnone \strike0\striked0 \
\pard\pardeftab720\pardirnatural\partightenfactor0
\cf0 6.4 FRQ 4 \'97 Conceptual Analysis (short, 4 pts)\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa120\pardirnatural\partightenfactor0

\f2\fs22 \cf3 Tests Science Practice 1 (Concept Explanation) most directly. The most common short FRQ archetype.
\f1\fs24 \cf0 \
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\sa60\pardirnatural\partightenfactor0
\ls7\ilvl0
\f4\i\b\fs22 \cf3 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\ul \strike \strikec3 Frame: Name the concept being tested explicitly. State the command verb. Surface the rubric criteria as a 4-item checklist.
\f1\i0\b0\fs24 \cf0 \ulnone \strike0\striked0 \
\ls7\ilvl0
\f4\i\b\fs22 \cf3 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\ul \strike \strikec3 Repair: 
\f5\i0\b0 For a missing-mechanism gap, default to Tighten with the bracket marker. For a missing-concept gap, default to Show. The D\uc0\u8594 E\u8594 P scaffold (Define \u8594  Explain \u8594  Predict) from the Blueprint_Teaching draft is the natural response structure here.
\f1\fs24 \cf0 \ulnone \strike0\striked0 \
\pard\pardeftab720\pardirnatural\partightenfactor0
\cf0 6.5 FRQ 5 \'97 Analyze Model or Visual Representation (short, 4 pts)\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\sa60\pardirnatural\partightenfactor0
\ls8\ilvl0
\f4\i\b\fs22 \cf3 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\ul \strike \strikec3 Frame: Surface what the visual is showing and what the rubric expects the student to read off it.
\f1\i0\b0\fs24 \cf0 \ulnone \strike0\striked0 \
\ls8\ilvl0
\f4\i\b\fs22 \cf3 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\ul \strike \strikec3 Repair: For a misread visual, default to Show with the reading move worked out. For a correct read but weak articulation, default to Tighten.
\f1\i0\b0\fs24 \cf0 \ulnone \strike0\striked0 \
\pard\pardeftab720\pardirnatural\partightenfactor0
\cf0 6.6 FRQ 6 \'97 Analyze Data (short, 4 pts)\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\sa60\pardirnatural\partightenfactor0
\ls9\ilvl0
\f4\i\b\fs22 \cf3 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\ul \strike \strikec3 Frame: State what the data shows and what the rubric expects (trend description, predicted value with units, explanation).
\f1\i0\b0\fs24 \cf0 \ulnone \strike0\striked0 \
\ls9\ilvl0
\f4\i\b\fs22 \cf3 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\ul \strike \strikec3 Repair: For a missed trend, Show. For a missed explanation, Tighten if the student has the concept and Show if not.
\f1\i0\b0\fs24 \cf0 \ulnone \strike0\striked0 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa120\pardirnatural\partightenfactor0
\cf0 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sb80\sa200\pardirnatural\partightenfactor0
\cf0 \
\pard\pardeftab720\pardirnatural\partightenfactor0
\cf0 7. Adaptation for Multiple-Choice\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa120\pardirnatural\partightenfactor0

\f2\fs22 \cf3 MCQ accounts for 50% of the exam score. The volume is substantial and the misconception diagnosis on a missed MCQ is high-yield because distractors are designed to surface common confusions.
\f1\fs24 \cf0 \
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\sa60\pardirnatural\partightenfactor0
\ls10\ilvl0
\f4\i\b\fs22 \cf3 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\ul \strike \strikec3 Frame: State the concept being tested and the distractor trap. Shorter than for FRQ \'97 typically a single sentence.
\f1\i0\b0\fs24 \cf0 \ulnone \strike0\striked0 \
\ls10\ilvl0
\f4\i\b\fs22 \cf3 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\ul \strike \strikec3 Attempt: Student selects an answer. The optional "why" field is critical \'97 it is what makes Repair targeted rather than generic.
\f1\i0\b0\fs24 \cf0 \ulnone \strike0\striked0 \
\ls10\ilvl0
\f4\i\b\fs22 \cf3 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\ul \strike \strikec3 Score: Right or wrong, plus an explanation of why each distractor was constructed. Even a correct answer is an opportunity to teach the trap.
\f1\i0\b0\fs24 \cf0 \ulnone \strike0\striked0 \
\ls10\ilvl0
\f4\i\b\fs22 \cf3 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\ul \strike \strikec3 Repair: For a chosen distractor that maps to a known misconception, Show with a worked example of the misconception's correct alternative. For an apparent misread, Tighten by pointing at the keyword the student missed.
\f1\i0\b0\fs24 \cf0 \ulnone \strike0\striked0 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa120\pardirnatural\partightenfactor0
\cf0 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sb80\sa200\pardirnatural\partightenfactor0
\cf0 \
\pard\pardeftab720\pardirnatural\partightenfactor0
\cf0 8. Student-Supplied Questions and Landing Pages\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa120\pardirnatural\partightenfactor0

\f2\fs22 \cf3 This is the highest-value interaction Cramapple has, and the primary acquisition channel. When a student pastes in a question they are actually stuck on \'97 homework, a practice exam, a prep book \'97 they have a real problem and have explicitly chosen Cramapple over ChatGPT. The flow that handles this moment is both a teaching moment for the individual student and a content artifact for SEO and AEO acquisition.
\f1\fs24 \cf0 \
\pard\pardeftab720\pardirnatural\partightenfactor0
\cf0 8.1 The Dual Purpose\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa120\pardirnatural\partightenfactor0

\f2\fs22 \cf3 Every resolved student-supplied question serves two functions at once. First, it teaches the individual student using the Score Loop adapted for an externally-provided question. Second, it becomes an anonymized public landing page \'97 a unique URL that ranks in search and gets cited by answer engines (ChatGPT, Perplexity, Gemini, Claude). The pages are the content moat: ChatGPT cannot match persistent, indexable, rubric-aware teaching artifacts because ChatGPT does not have stable URLs that search engines can index. Over time the corpus of landing pages becomes a long-tail acquisition surface that compounds.
\f1\fs24 \cf0 \

\f2\fs22 \cf3 The dual purpose raises the quality bar on every student-supplied interaction. Generic, rubric-recycled feedback \'97 the dominant failure mode in the AI grading literature (Section 5.3) \'97 was already a pedagogical problem. With landing pages, it is also a marketing problem. Pages with weak teaching do not rank, do not get cited, do not acquire. The criterion-specific, evidence-grounded feedback the Score Loop produces is what makes both the student moment and the public page work.
\f1\fs24 \cf0 \
\pard\pardeftab720\pardirnatural\partightenfactor0
\cf0 8.2 Pre-Frame: Matching, Confidence, and Portfolio Check\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa120\pardirnatural\partightenfactor0

\f2\fs22 \cf3 Before Cramapple does anything, it categorizes the question through three layered checks:
\f1\fs24 \cf0 \
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\sa60\pardirnatural\partightenfactor0
\ls11\ilvl0
\f4\i\b\fs22 \cf3 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\ul \strike \strikec3 Known-question match. Is this question in Cramapple's existing corpus? Released FRQs, Cramapple-authored questions, prior student-supplied questions. Exact match or high-similarity match triggers high-confidence treatment.
\f1\i0\b0\fs24 \cf0 \ulnone \strike0\striked0 \
\ls11\ilvl0
\f4\i\b\fs22 \cf3 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\ul \strike \strikec3 Portfolio check. Does this question fall within the set of subjects Cramapple covers? This is intentionally broader than the student's currently enrolled subject. A Bio-enrolled student can paste a chemistry question; if chemistry is in Cramapple's portfolio, that triggers a different flow than if it is not.
\f1\i0\b0\fs24 \cf0 \ulnone \strike0\striked0 \
\ls11\ilvl0
\f4\i\b\fs22 \cf3 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\ul \strike \strikec3 Categorization. Which subject, Science Practice (or equivalent for non-Bio), Unit, and archetype does the question most plausibly map to? This becomes the seed for Frame.
\f1\i0\b0\fs24 \cf0 \ulnone \strike0\striked0 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa120\pardirnatural\partightenfactor0

\f2\fs22 \cf3 Each check produces a confidence score. The combined confidence drives the rest of the flow. Cramapple surfaces the categorization to the student for confirmation: *"This looks like an AP Bio Conceptual Analysis question about gene regulation. Sound right?"* The student can correct or confirm. The act of categorizing is itself pedagogically valuable \'97 it trains the student in archetype recognition, a skill they need on exam day.
\f1\fs24 \cf0 \
\pard\pardeftab720\pardirnatural\partightenfactor0
\cf0 8.3 Clarification Prompts\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa120\pardirnatural\partightenfactor0

\f2\fs22 \cf3 Two specific failure modes trigger a clarification prompt rather than a flat decline. The student gets one round to fix the issue before Cramapple proceeds or declines.
\f1\fs24 \cf0 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa120\pardirnatural\partightenfactor0

\f0\b\fs22 \cf3 Relevance failure. 
\f2\b0 The question appears outside the current scope or in a different subject. Example prompt: "This looks more like a chemistry question. Was that intentional? Some AP Bio questions touch on biochemistry, so I want to make sure I'm reading it right." The student confirms, redirects, or refines.
\f1\fs24 \cf0 \

\f0\b\fs22 \cf3 Completeness failure. 
\f2\b0 The question references a figure, data table, or passage that was not included. Example prompt: "This question mentions a graph I don't see. Want to paste the figure or describe what it shows?" Common cause: OCR errors on photographed questions, or partial copy-paste from a multi-part FRQ.
\f1\fs24 \cf0 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa120\pardirnatural\partightenfactor0

\f2\fs22 \cf3 One clarification round is the limit. If the student does not resolve the issue, Cramapple proceeds with appropriately hedged confidence or politely declines. Forced clarification loops degrade the experience and are not pedagogically useful.
\f1\fs24 \cf0 \
\pard\pardeftab720\pardirnatural\partightenfactor0
