\f2\fs22 \cf3 Surfacing the mode choice is itself a demonstration that Cramapple is more pedagogically considered than ChatGPT. ChatGPT gives whatever you asked for; Cramapple makes the meta-choice visible because the meta-choice has learning consequences.
\f1\fs24 \cf0 \
\pard\pardeftab720\pardirnatural\partightenfactor0
\cf0 8.5 Confidence-Modulated Score Loop\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa120\pardirnatural\partightenfactor0

\f2\fs22 \cf3 When the student picks Check My Work (or Teach Me, or any mode that runs the full or partial loop), the four-beat shape stays constant but every beat is modulated by the matching confidence:
\f1\fs24 \cf0 \
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\sa60\pardirnatural\partightenfactor0
\ls12\ilvl0
\f4\i\b\fs22 \cf3 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\ul \strike \strikec3 Frame becomes interpretive. High confidence (matched known question): Frame states the rubric criteria as if Cramapple-authored. Moderate confidence: Frame surfaces the assumption. ("Based on the command verb and the question type, the rubric most likely awards points for X, Y, Z.") Low confidence: Frame is more conversational and does not commit to specific criteria.
\f1\i0\b0\fs24 \cf0 \ulnone \strike0\striked0 \
\ls12\ilvl0
\f4\i\b\fs22 \cf3 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\ul \strike \strikec3 Score uses an inferred rubric. The Score panel honestly presents the source of the rubric. "Based on AP Biology rubric patterns for Conceptual Analysis FRQs, here's how this would likely be evaluated." Not "You earned 3 of 4 points" with false precision.
\f1\i0\b0\fs24 \cf0 \ulnone \strike0\striked0 \
\ls12\ilvl0
\f4\i\b\fs22 \cf3 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\ul \strike \strikec3 Repair adapts to confidence. High confidence runs Repair normally. Moderate makes the diagnostic hedged. Low confidence becomes more conversational and offers the student multiple framings.
\f1\i0\b0\fs24 \cf0 \ulnone \strike0\striked0 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa120\pardirnatural\partightenfactor0

\f2\fs22 \cf3 Honesty about confidence is a competitive advantage. ChatGPT presents everything with the same confident tone whether the underlying answer is right or wrong. Cramapple's willingness to say "I'm moderately confident this is about gene regulation; the rubric pattern suggests three criteria" is both pedagogically more useful and brand-differentiating.
\f1\fs24 \cf0 \
\pard\pardeftab720\pardirnatural\partightenfactor0
\cf0 8.6 Two Flow Variants: Enrolled vs. Visitor\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa120\pardirnatural\partightenfactor0

\f2\fs22 \cf3 The same question paste produces different flows depending on whether the user is an enrolled student or a landing-page visitor. The matching matters for handling subject-portfolio edge cases.
\f1\fs24 \cf0 \
\pard\pardeftab720\pardirnatural\partightenfactor0
\cf0 Enrolled student\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa120\pardirnatural\partightenfactor0

\f2\fs22 \cf3 A student who has purchased Cramapple for a specific subject pastes a question. Four cases:
\f1\fs24 \cf0 \
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\sa60\pardirnatural\partightenfactor0
\ls13\ilvl0
\f5\fs22 \cf3 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\ul \strike \strikec3 Question matches enrolled subject \uc0\u8594  standard flow with the four modes.
\f1\fs24 \cf0 \ulnone \strike0\striked0 \
\ls13\ilvl0
\f5\fs22 \cf3 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\ul \strike \strikec3 Question matches a different subject in Cramapple's portfolio \uc0\u8594  Cramapple answers the question and offers a subject expansion. The teaching does not get withheld; the upsell sits alongside. Functional intent: "I'll teach you this one, and if you'd like ongoing coverage of [other subject], you can extend your package."
\f1\fs24 \cf0 \ulnone \strike0\striked0 \
\ls13\ilvl0
\f5\fs22 \cf3 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\ul \strike \strikec3 Question is outside Cramapple's portfolio entirely \uc0\u8594  polite decline plus waitlist offer per Section 8.7. "This looks like [French/music theory/etc.]. I'm not built for that subject yet. Want me to let you know when I am?"
\f1\fs24 \cf0 \ulnone \strike0\striked0 \
\ls13\ilvl0
\f5\fs22 \cf3 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\ul \strike \strikec3 Question fails relevance/completeness check \uc0\u8594  clarification prompt per Section 8.3.
\f1\fs24 \cf0 \ulnone \strike0\striked0 \
\pard\pardeftab720\pardirnatural\partightenfactor0
\cf0 Landing page visitor\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa120\pardirnatural\partightenfactor0

\f2\fs22 \cf3 A visitor arrives via search engine, AEO citation, or social share to a specific landing page. They see the original question and Cramapple's teaching. Then they may ask their own question. Three cases:
\f1\fs24 \cf0 \
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\sa60\pardirnatural\partightenfactor0
\ls14\ilvl0
\f5\fs22 \cf3 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\ul \strike \strikec3 Question matches any subject in Cramapple's portfolio \uc0\u8594  Cramapple answers normally and makes the broader portfolio visible. "I covered this; here are the other subjects I teach if you're prepping for more than one AP exam."
\f1\fs24 \cf0 \ulnone \strike0\striked0 \
\ls14\ilvl0
\f5\fs22 \cf3 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\ul \strike \strikec3 Question is outside Cramapple's portfolio entirely \uc0\u8594  polite decline plus waitlist offer per Section 8.7.
\f1\fs24 \cf0 \ulnone \strike0\striked0 \
\ls14\ilvl0
\f5\fs22 \cf3 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\ul \strike \strikec3 Question fails relevance/completeness check \uc0\u8594  clarification prompt.
\f1\fs24 \cf0 \ulnone \strike0\striked0 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa120\pardirnatural\partightenfactor0

\f2\fs22 \cf3 The asymmetry between enrolled and visitor is important. The enrolled student is paying and has chosen a subject; their off-topic question is an upsell moment. The visitor has chosen nothing yet; their off-topic question is a discovery moment. Both are handled gracefully; neither is treated as a failure.
\f1\fs24 \cf0 \
\pard\pardeftab720\pardirnatural\partightenfactor0
\cf0 8.7 Subject Waitlist: Capturing Demand for Unsupported Subjects\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa120\pardirnatural\partightenfactor0

\f2\fs22 \cf3 Every off-portfolio decline is a moment of genuine intent. The visitor or student has a real question on a real subject, has chosen Cramapple over the alternatives, and we have to say no. Letting that moment end with a flat decline wastes both the relationship and the demand signal. The waitlist mechanism turns the decline into a soft yes and captures three distinct pieces of value at once: a future customer, a marketing contact, and a product-prioritization data point.
\f1\fs24 \cf0 \
\pard\pardeftab720\pardirnatural\partightenfactor0
\cf0 Functional intent\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa120\pardirnatural\partightenfactor0

\f2\fs22 \cf3 When a question fails the portfolio check (the subject is not one Cramapple covers), the decline message includes an explicit offer: notification when Cramapple launches that subject. The student provides phone or email \'97 their choice, lower friction than requiring both \'97 and the contact goes into a per-subject waitlist. When Cramapple launches the subject, the waitlist receives an announcement. The promise is specific and bounded: we will tell you when we have this; we will not flood you with unrelated marketing.
\f1\fs24 \cf0 \
\pard\pardeftab720\pardirnatural\partightenfactor0
\cf0 What gets collected\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\sa60\pardirnatural\partightenfactor0
\ls15\ilvl0
\f4\i\b\fs22 \cf3 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\ul \strike \strikec3 Contact method (phone or email, student's choice).
\f1\i0\b0\fs24 \cf0 \ulnone \strike0\striked0 \
\ls15\ilvl0
\f4\i\b\fs22 \cf3 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\ul \strike \strikec3 Requested subject (inferred from the categorization in Section 8.2; confirmed by the student).
\f1\i0\b0\fs24 \cf0 \ulnone \strike0\striked0 \
\ls15\ilvl0
\f4\i\b\fs22 \cf3 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\ul \strike \strikec3 Timestamp.
\f1\i0\b0\fs24 \cf0 \ulnone \strike0\striked0 \
\ls15\ilvl0
\f4\i\b\fs22 \cf3 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\ul \strike \strikec3 Source (enrolled-student vs landing-page-visitor, used for downstream segmentation).
\f1\i0\b0\fs24 \cf0 \ulnone \strike0\striked0 \
\ls15\ilvl0
\f4\i\b\fs22 \cf3 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\ul \strike \strikec3 Whether the contact is the student's own or a parent's (relevant to COPPA \'97 see below).
\f1\i0\b0\fs24 \cf0 \ulnone \strike0\striked0 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa120\pardirnatural\partightenfactor0

\f2\fs22 \cf3 Nothing else. No personal demographic data, no school information, no payment context. The minimal-collection posture is consistent with Section 13 and reduces compliance surface.
\f1\fs24 \cf0 \
\pard\pardeftab720\pardirnatural\partightenfactor0
\cf0 COPPA handling\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa120\pardirnatural\partightenfactor0

\f2\fs22 \cf3 Cramapple does not know the age of a landing-page visitor. Collecting contact information from a user under 13 without verifiable parental consent is a COPPA violation. The waitlist form addresses this by presenting the collection as either-or: "Want us to let you know? You or a parent can leave a phone number or email." The form asks the user to confirm whether they are 13 or older. Under-13 users are prompted to provide a parent's contact rather than their own; over-13 users may provide their own. This is the lightest-friction COPPA-compliant pattern and defers heavier verifiable-parental-consent infrastructure until launch volume requires it. The specific form copy and compliance pattern should be reviewed by counsel before launch.
\f1\fs24 \cf0 \
\pard\pardeftab720\pardirnatural\partightenfactor0
\cf0 How the data is used\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa120\pardirnatural\partightenfactor0

\f2\fs22 \cf3 Three distinct uses:
\f1\fs24 \cf0 \
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\sa60\pardirnatural\partightenfactor0
\ls16\ilvl0
\f4\i\b\fs22 \cf3 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\ul \strike \strikec3 Subject prioritization. Aggregate waitlist counts by subject become an evidence-based prioritization signal for the product roadmap. If AP Chemistry has 5,000 waitlist entries and AP Music Theory has 80, the roadmap reflects that. This converts the qualitative "what should we build next" question into a quantitative ranking driven by real demand.
\f1\i0\b0\fs24 \cf0 \ulnone \strike0\striked0 \
\ls16\ilvl0
\f4\i\b\fs22 \cf3 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\ul \strike \strikec3 Launch marketing. When a subject launches, the corresponding waitlist receives an announcement. Conversion from a waitlist that signed up specifically for that subject should outperform cold marketing by a large margin \'97 these are users who explicitly raised their hand.
\f1\i0\b0\fs24 \cf0 \ulnone \strike0\striked0 \
\ls16\ilvl0
\f4\i\b\fs22 \cf3 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\ul \strike \strikec3 Adjacent-subject upsell. Enrolled students who signed up for a waitlist on a different Cramapple-covered subject (the upsell case in Section 8.6) can be re-engaged when they finish their current subject. The waitlist entry is itself a high-intent buying signal.
\f1\i0\b0\fs24 \cf0 \ulnone \strike0\striked0 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa120\pardirnatural\partightenfactor0

\f2\fs22 \cf3 Each communication includes a clear unsubscribe mechanism per CAN-SPAM and standard practice. The waitlist is a contractual promise (we said we'd tell you when X launches), not a general marketing channel; we honor that distinction.
\f1\fs24 \cf0 \
\pard\pardeftab720\pardirnatural\partightenfactor0
\cf0 Operational implications\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa120\pardirnatural\partightenfactor0

\f2\fs22 \cf3 The mechanism requires three pieces of infrastructure to ship at launch: a waitlist data model (per-subject table with the fields listed above), a simple form attached to the off-portfolio decline flow, and a launch-announcement pipeline tied to subject release. None of these are heavy lift; the work is making sure they actually fire from the right moments in the user flow. The reporting side \'97 aggregate waitlist counts by subject, surfaced to the roadmap process \'97 is a small admin view that becomes the input to release planning.
\f1\fs24 \cf0 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa80\pardirnatural\partightenfactor0

\f3\i\fs18 \cf4 Decision: subject waitlist ships at MVP launch. Form collection is contact-of-user-or-parent with explicit age check. Subject prioritization based on waitlist counts becomes a standing input to release planning.
\f1\i0\fs24 \cf0 \

\f3\i\fs18 \cf4 Open: exact form copy and COPPA-compliant pattern (counsel review needed before launch); the cadence of internal reporting on waitlist counts; whether waitlist entries also feed into customer-development conversations (e.g., interview prospects who signed up for the same not-yet-launched subject).
\f1\i0\fs24 \cf0 \
\pard\pardeftab720\pardirnatural\partightenfactor0
\cf0 8.8 Quality Gate for Landing Page Publishing\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa120\pardirnatural\partightenfactor0

\f2\fs22 \cf3 Not every student-supplied question produces a landing page. A quality gate sits between the resolved question and publication, mirroring the quality framework Cramapple uses for canonical Orly-authored questions.
\f1\fs24 \cf0 \
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\sa60\pardirnatural\partightenfactor0
\ls17\ilvl0
\f4\i\b\fs22 \cf3 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\ul \strike \strikec3 Above quality threshold: Cramapple answers the student normally AND publishes the question and teaching as a landing page.
\f1\i0\b0\fs24 \cf0 \ulnone \strike0\striked0 \
\ls17\ilvl0
\f4\i\b\fs22 \cf3 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\ul \strike \strikec3 Below quality threshold: Cramapple may still answer the student (they get value) but does not publish a page. Low-quality questions, malformed questions, or questions Cramapple's confidence cannot support are not exposed publicly.
\f1\i0\b0\fs24 \cf0 \ulnone \strike0\striked0 \
\ls17\ilvl0
\f4\i\b\fs22 \cf3 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\ul \strike \strikec3 Borderline: Queued for review. Orly or a content reviewer assesses; possibly edits the rendered version for relevance and quality before publication.
\f1\i0\b0\fs24 \cf0 \ulnone \strike0\striked0 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa120\pardirnatural\partightenfactor0

\f2\fs22 \cf3 The quality gate has two functions. It protects the SEO and AEO surface from low-quality content that would degrade Cramapple's domain authority. And it preserves Cramapple's pedagogical credibility \'97 every public page is one Cramapple stands behind. The same scoring rubric Orly uses to evaluate canonical content applies.
\f1\fs24 \cf0 \
\pard\pardeftab720\pardirnatural\partightenfactor0
\cf0 8.9 Landing Page Experience\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa120\pardirnatural\partightenfactor0

\f2\fs22 \cf3 A landing page renders the question, Cramapple's Frame (criteria the AP rubric awards), and the teaching that resolved the question. The visitor sees the value before any paywall. Two interactive surfaces follow:
\f1\fs24 \cf0 \
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\sa60\pardirnatural\partightenfactor0
\ls18\ilvl0
\f4\i\b\fs22 \cf3 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\ul \strike \strikec3 "Have another question?" The visitor can paste their own question. One free question without signup. This is the trial.
\f1\i0\b0\fs24 \cf0 \ulnone \strike0\striked0 \
\ls18\ilvl0
\f4\i\b\fs22 \cf3 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\ul \strike \strikec3 "Want to put this lesson to the test?" A second, related question on the same skill that uses the teaching the visitor just received. The visitor applies the lesson immediately. If Cramapple can show outcomes ("students who took the test got it right 73% of the time"), the conversion lever is stronger.
\f1\i0\b0\fs24 \cf0 \ulnone \strike0\striked0 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa120\pardirnatural\partightenfactor0

\f2\fs22 \cf3 The "put this lesson to the test" surface is the more pedagogically interesting of the two. It demonstrates that Cramapple's teaching produces transferable skill, not just an answer to a single question. This is the structural argument against ChatGPT \'97 generic AI gives you the answer; Cramapple gives you the skill, and proves it on a second question.
\f1\fs24 \cf0 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa120\pardirnatural\partightenfactor0

\f0\b\fs22 \cf3 Disclosure. 
\f2\b0 A simple note at the point of submission: "We use this anonymously to help other students." No opt-in or opt-out machinery. The landing page contains no identifying information about the student who originated the question. The student may share their landing page if they choose; that is their decision.
\f1\fs24 \cf0 \
\pard\pardeftab720\pardirnatural\partightenfactor0
\cf0 8.10 The Share Loop\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa120\pardirnatural\partightenfactor0

\f2\fs22 \cf3 One free question per share. The student or visitor who shares the landing page to social (Discord, group chat, Snapchat, Instagram, TikTok, X) earns one additional free question. The mechanic is intentionally simple: testable conversion, predictable inference cost, easy to communicate.
\f1\fs24 \cf0 \

\f2\fs22 \cf3 Some users will game the system (fake shares, throwaway accounts). The marginal inference cost of an extra free question is low enough that gaming is acceptable. The signal value of even a gamed share \'97 an additional URL in social \'97 is positive for the acquisition surface. ECONOMICS.docx should model the marginal cost of share-unlocked questions against the conversion-to-paid rate; that economic analysis is separate from this document but flagged here as the relevant downstream artifact.
\f1\fs24 \cf0 \
\pard\pardeftab720\pardirnatural\partightenfactor0
\cf0 8.11 Anti-Scraping\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa120\pardirnatural\partightenfactor0

\f2\fs22 \cf3 Landing pages are the content moat and need protection from bulk extraction without introducing user friction. The vision is: search engines (Google, Bing) crawl freely so SEO works; answer engines (ChatGPT, Perplexity, Gemini, Claude) can index responsibly; known training scrapers and competitor bulk-extractors are blocked; the typical student never sees a challenge.
\f1\fs24 \cf0 \

\f2\fs22 \cf3 Functional requirements (implementation belongs to engineering and infrastructure):
\f1\fs24 \cf0 \
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\sa60\pardirnatural\partightenfactor0
\ls19\ilvl0
\f4\i\b\fs22 \cf3 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\ul \strike \strikec3 No CAPTCHA. The annoyance cost is greater than the protection value for our use case.
\f1\i0\b0\fs24 \cf0 \ulnone \strike0\striked0 \
\ls19\ilvl0
\f4\i\b\fs22 \cf3 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\ul \strike \strikec3 Invisible challenge for suspicious traffic (Cloudflare Turnstile or equivalent). Fires only on bot-like signals; legitimate users never see it.
\f1\i0\b0\fs24 \cf0 \ulnone \strike0\striked0 \
\ls19\ilvl0
\f4\i\b\fs22 \cf3 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\ul \strike \strikec3 Rate limiting per IP and per session. Bulk access patterns get throttled.
\f1\i0\b0\fs24 \cf0 \ulnone \strike0\striked0 \
\ls19\ilvl0
\f4\i\b\fs22 \cf3 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\ul \strike \strikec3 robots.txt explicitly allowing major search crawlers and denying known training scrapers. This list moves; Orly and engineering should maintain it.
\f1\i0\b0\fs24 \cf0 \ulnone \strike0\striked0 \
\ls19\ilvl0
\f4\i\b\fs22 \cf3 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\ul \strike \strikec3 The gating itself \'97 one free question, share-to-unlock \'97 acts as a natural anti-bulk-extraction mechanism. The product economics already limit how much value any single visitor can extract.
\f1\i0\b0\fs24 \cf0 \ulnone \strike0\striked0 \
\pard\pardeftab720\pardirnatural\partightenfactor0
\cf0 8.12 Lock Integration\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa120\pardirnatural\partightenfactor0

\f2\fs22 \cf3 For enrolled students, student-supplied questions enter the Lock queue only when confidence is high enough to identify the underlying Science Practice and Unit. Low-confidence questions do not feed the next-best-action engine \'97 using a misclassified question to drive future recommendations would degrade the diagnostic.
\f1\fs24 \cf0 \

\f2\fs22 \cf3 For landing page visitors who have not enrolled, there is no Lock queue. The interaction is one-off (or two-off, with the bonus question). Conversion to enrollment is what creates a Lock queue going forward.
\f1\fs24 \cf0 \
\pard\pardeftab720\pardirnatural\partightenfactor0
